# Local development.
#
# The public site connects READ-ONLY through this URL and relies on row-level
# security for isolation, so pointing it at a database with other tenants in it
# is safe by design. It is still a local database only.
# Port 5433, not 5432: a pre-existing PostgreSQL service on the host commonly
# holds 5432, and the connection silently goes there instead of to Docker.
#   docker run -d --name forecourt-db -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=postgres \
#     -e POSTGRES_DB=forecourt -p 5433:5432 postgres:16
DATABASE_URL=postgres://postgres:postgres@localhost:5433/forecourt

# Optional. Renders the finance block locally against a DEMO compliance
# sign-off. Never set this anywhere a customer can reach.
# DEMO_SIGN_COMPLIANCE_RULE=1

# The dev server's port. `next dev` reads this from the real environment, not
# from this file, so to change it for one run:
#   PowerShell:  $env:PORT=3001; pnpm dev
#   bash/zsh:    PORT=3001 pnpm dev
# PORT=3000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- deployment
#
# Nothing below belongs in a local .env — it is here so the deployment's
# variables are documented in the same place as the local ones.
#
# On Railway each service needs exactly two:
#
#   APP=crm | site | admin       selects which app the shared Dockerfile builds
#   DATABASE_URL=${{Postgres.DATABASE_URL}}
#                                a REFERENCE to the database service, so it
#                                follows the database if it moves
#
# Do NOT set PORT — Railway injects it and the standalone server reads it.
#
# To run migrations from your machine, use Railway's PUBLIC url and add
# sslmode, because the proxy's certificate is not in Node's trust store:
#
#   DATABASE_URL='postgresql://...@turntable.proxy.rlwy.net:PORT/railway?sslmode=require' \
#     pnpm db:deploy
#
# See docs/12-deploying-to-railway.md.
